    "result_text": "Done. The full suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`), including the new `namesakeCount` acceptance test.\n\n## What I changed\n\nThe rule: on create, `namesakeCount` = the number of existing owners (before this create) sharing the same first + last name, case-insensitively \u2014 persisted and returned on the owner.\n\nMinimal-impact edits:\n\n1. **`Namesakes.java`** (new, package-private helper mirroring the existing `Households`) \u2014 a tiny cohesive unit with zero surrounding complexity that does the counting.\n2. **`OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`** \u2014 a single added line: `owner.setNamesakeCount(Namesakes.count(owners, owner));`, reusing the already-fetched `owners` collection.\n3. **`Owner.java`** \u2014 one `namesake_count` field plus a trivial getter/setter (MapStruct picks it up by name, so no mapper change).\n4. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 a read-only `namesakeCount` property on the `Owner` schema so the generated `OwnerDto` exposes it.\n5. **`db/hsqldb/schema.sql`** + **`data.sql`** \u2014 the new column and a trailing `NULL` on the 10 positional seed inserts.\n\nI deliberately put the logic in a new low-complexity helper and touched `addOwner` with just one line, since that method sits in a large class (high WMC_other) where each changed line is the expensive part \u2014 a new tiny file costs far less than growing that method.",
